    {
      "body": "Docker isn't installed on this machine. There's no Podman either, and your user isn't in a `docker` group. You're on Debian sid (forky), and Docker's official apt repo doesn't support sid well, so Debian's own package is the simplest route:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt update\nsudo apt install docker.io docker-buildx docker-compose\nsudo systemctl enable --now docker\nsudo usermod -aG docker $USER   # lets you run docker without sudo\n```\n\nThen log out and back in, or run `newgrp docker` in your current shell, so the group change applies. Check it works with:\n\n```bash\ndocker run --rm hello-world\n```\n\nI haven't run any of this myself because it needs `sudo` and changes your system. You can run it in your terminal, or type `! <command>` here.\n\nOne caution: adding yourself to the `docker` group effectively gives you root access. That's the usual setup on a personal dev machine. If you'd rather avoid it, skip the `usermod` line and use `sudo docker ...` instead.\n\nOnce Docker is working, tell me which part of the 05-deployment homework you're on and I can help with the Dockerfile or the commands.",
      "id": "209191ea-f1c2-4490-b608-9c8f62b1f8ad",
      "time": 1793035264.5162945,
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "type": "msg",
      "mentions": [],
      "raw_time": false
    },
