    {
      "body": "Yes, you can answer the whole homework without installing Docker. Only Q6 actually uses it, and for that one you can get the answer but not test the container itself.\n\n| Question | Needs Docker? | How to answer it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Q1: uv version | No | `uv --version` |\n| Q2: Locked scikit-learn version | No | Look up `scikit-learn` in `uv.lock` |\n| Q3: Load the model with pickle | No | `uv run python q3_load_model.py` |\n| Q4: Serve with FastAPI | No | `uv run uvicorn predict:app --port 9696`, then POST the lead |\n| Q5: Python base image | No | Read it from the `FROM` line in `Dockerfile`: `python:3.11.15-slim-bookworm` |\n| Q6: Run the container | Yes, to really do it | See below |\n\n**Q6:** your notebook says the container \"serves the same frozen artifact, so the answer must match Q4.\" The image runs the same `pipeline.bin` with the same `predict.py`, so the prediction is the same as Q4's. You can also run the Q6 client against your local server:\n\n```bash\nuv run uvicorn predict:app --port 9696 &\nuv run python q6_test.py http://localhost:9696/predict\n```\n\nThat shows what the container would return, but it doesn't prove the `Dockerfile` builds and runs. If you want to check that without installing anything, you could run it in GitHub Codespaces, which has Docker already installed.",
