    {
      "body": "Docker is now installed and the daemon is running. The error is a permissions problem: the Docker socket can only be used by the `docker` group, and your user isn't in it yet (the group currently lists only `root`).\n\nAdd yourself to the group:\n\n```bash\nsudo usermod -aG docker $USER\n```\n\nThe change only applies to new login sessions, so either log out and back in, or apply it to your current terminal with:\n\n```bash\nnewgrp docker\n```\n\nThen check it works:\n\n```bash\ndocker run --rm hello-world\n```\n\nSame caution as before: being in the `docker` group effectively gives you root access. If you'd rather not, skip the group change and run everything as `sudo docker ...`.\n\nOnce that works, this answers Q6 (run from `05-deployment/solutions`):\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t hw5 .\ndocker run -d --rm -p 9696:9696 --name hw5 hw5\nuv run python q6_test.py\ndocker stop hw5\n```\n\nIf your local uvicorn from Q4 is still running on port 9696, stop it first or the `docker run` will fail because the port is taken.",
      "id": "e041dc28-3abf-49b2-9028-880144e1b420",
