    {
      "body": "Your user still hasn't been added to the `docker` group. Its only member is `root`. That usually means `usermod` ran with `$USER` set to `root`, which happens if you ran it inside a root shell (`sudo su` or `sudo -i`), so root got added instead of you.\n\nRun it from your normal terminal (not a root shell) with your username written out:\n\n```bash\nsudo usermod -aG docker alfredo\ngetent group docker      # should now show: docker:x:982:root,alfredo\n```\n\nThen apply the change. Opening a new terminal tab won't do it; you need either:\n\n```bash\nnewgrp docker            # applies to this terminal only\n```\n\nor a full log out and back in, which applies everywhere, including the terminal Claude Code runs in.\n\nThen check:\n\n```bash\ndocker run --rm hello-world\n```\n\nIf you just want to get Q6 done now, `sudo docker run --rm hello-world` (and `sudo docker build ...` etc.) works without any group change.",
      "id": "501e44a7-8bf1-4dfd-96fd-1a89ba48a686",
      "time": 1793036253.124188,
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "type": "msg",
      "mentions": [],
      "raw_time": false
    },
