    {
      "body": "Roughly 1\u20133 minutes the first time, depending on your internet speed. After that it's much faster.\n\n- **`docker build`, first run:** it downloads the `python:3.11-slim` base image and the `uv` image (about 60 MB together), then `uv sync` installs FastAPI, scikit-learn, NumPy and SciPy (about 100 MB or more). Most of the time goes to those downloads.\n- **`docker build`, later runs:** a few seconds, because the layers are cached. Changing your `.py` files doesn't trigger a reinstall; only changes to `pyproject.toml` or `uv.lock` do.\n- **`docker run -d`:** about one second. It prints the container ID and returns, and the server is ready a second or two later.\n\nTwo things to watch for:\n\n1. **`-q` hides all progress**, so the cell will look frozen until it finishes. If you want to see what's happening, drop `-q`.\n2. **There's no `.dockerignore`**, so Docker sends your 244 MB `.venv` to the daemon as build context every time, even though the Dockerfile never copies it. Creating a `.dockerignore` next to the Dockerfile skips that:\n   ```\n   .venv\n   __pycache__\n   .ipynb_checkpoints\n   ```\n\nAlso, since you're running these from Jupyter with `!`: if the Jupyter server was started before you were added to the `docker` group, the kernel will still get the same `permission denied` error. If that happens, restart Jupyter from a shell where `docker run hello-world` works.\n\nWhen the container is up, check it with `!uv run python q6_test.py` and stop it with `!docker stop hw5`.",
      "id": "86de4a53-48cb-436d-8cd5-8ce822c02e26",
